WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.580
Serialization is how Java applications
turn data or objects into a form that can be

2
00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:07.970
stored, transmitted, cached, queued,
logged, or written to disk.

3
00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:10.238
Deserialization is the reverse operation:

4
00:00:10.318 --> 00:00:13.984
the application reconstructs data or
objects from that representation.

5
00:00:14.064 --> 00:00:16.783
Those operations sound routine, and they
are routine,

6
00:00:16.863 --> 00:00:18.992
but they sit directly on an input
boundary.

7
00:00:19.072 --> 00:00:21.319
When a Java service accepts serialized
data,

8
00:00:21.399 --> 00:00:25.183
it is deciding what external bytes are
allowed to become application state.

9
00:00:25.263 --> 00:00:28.041
Modern Java systems deserialize data from
many places.

10
00:00:28.121 --> 00:00:30.486
A web service receives JSON from an API
request.

11
00:00:30.566 --> 00:00:32.399
A worker reads a message from a queue.

12
00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:33.484
A partner sends XML.

13
00:00:33.564 --> 00:00:35.220
A configuration tool reads YAML.

14
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:36.896
A session store restores state.

15
00:00:36.976 --> 00:00:39.282
A batch job imports files from object
storage.

16
00:00:39.362 --> 00:00:42.318
A cache returns values created earlier by
another service.

17
00:00:42.398 --> 00:00:43.817
Each path may look ordinary,

18
00:00:43.897 --> 00:00:46.912
but each one reconstructs data that code
may trust and act on.

19
00:00:46.992 --> 00:00:50.895
Unsafe deserialization can create many
kinds of harm: unauthorized actions,

20
00:00:50.975 --> 00:00:54.640
object confusion, validation bypass,
excessive resource consumption,

21
00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.800
sensitive data exposure, or execution of
code paths the developer did not intend.

22
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:03.078
Native Java object serialization is
especially sensitive when the data is not fully

23
00:01:03.158 --> 00:01:07.414
trusted because it can rebuild complex
object graphs and involve class behavior from

24
00:01:07.494 --> 00:01:09.446
the application and its dependencies.

25
00:01:09.526 --> 00:01:11.004
But ordinary JSON, XML, YAML,

26
00:01:11.084 --> 00:01:14.158
and binary formats can also be risky when
configured broadly.

27
00:01:14.238 --> 00:01:17.726
The goal is to serialize intentionally and
deserialize defensively.

28
00:01:17.806 --> 00:01:20.053
A safe design identifies the trust
boundary,

29
00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:22.380
chooses a format appropriate to the use
case,

30
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:25.298
constrains the allowed types, validates
the data shape,

31
00:01:25.378 --> 00:01:28.984
enforces authentication and authorization,
applies resource limits,

32
00:01:29.064 --> 00:01:31.311
avoids sensitive output leaks, logs
safely,

33
00:01:31.391 --> 00:01:35.175
and gives reviewers a clear way to reason
about what data is allowed to become

34
00:01:35.255 --> 00:01:36.260
application state.
